  "result": "All four tests pass.\n\n**What I did:** Implemented `editor/buffer.py` with a `TextBuffer` class backed by an undo stack and a redo stack of small command records. Each record stores the position, the text involved, and whether it was an insert or delete. Undo pops a record, applies its inverse, and moves it to the redo stack. Redo does the reverse. Any new edit clears the redo stack. Bounds checks raise `IndexError` before any state changes, including for negative positions and negative delete counts.\n\n**Verification:**\n\n```\npython -m pytest -q\n4 passed in 0.03s\n```\n\nNote that the machine has Python 3.14 rather than 3.12, but the code uses only features available in 3.10 and up, so it runs on either.\n\n**Approach chosen:** Approach A, the command pattern. Since the spec requires unbounded history, storing only the edited text per operation keeps memory proportional to the edits rather than to buffer size times history length, and inverting an insert into a delete of the same range is simple enough to be trivially correct.",
